"The letters 'Alif-Lam-Ra' at the beginning of Surah Hud, just like those at the beginning of other surahs in the Mushaf, point to the miraculous nature of the Quran. This surah is a book whose verses have been made decisive (muhkam) and clearly differentiated (mufassal). Muhkam means protected from corruption, sound, and full of wisdom; mufassal means explained, divided into sections, and detailed. These two qualities complement each other: the Quran is both utterly sound and explains every matter in detail.\n\nIn the first section of the verses, people are commanded to worship Allah alone and not to recognize any deity other than Him. Our Prophet (peace be upon him) was sent as a warner and a bearer of glad tidings. People are asked to seek forgiveness from their Lord and to turn to Him in repentance. Repentance (tawbah) and seeking forgiveness (istighfar) serve as a means for the pardon of sins and enable a person to lead a good life. Allah promises that He will grant a great reward to those who believe and perform righteous deeds.\n\nFollowing this call to seeking forgiveness and repentance, glad tidings are given that those of virtue will be given the reward of their virtue. Virtue is not merely fulfilling obligatory duties (fard), but also drawing closer to Allah through supererogatory acts of worship (nafilah) and being beneficial to people. An increase in the number of those who repent in society contributes to the spread of a sense of virtue; thus, there is also a reward in this world, but the true reward is in the Hereafter.\n\nIf people turn away, the Prophet fears for them the punishment of a tremendous day. That tremendous day is the Day of Resurrection. The return of everyone is to Allah; whether they repent or not, they will ultimately arrive in Allah's presence. In the verse, the disbelievers' attempts to hide from Allah are described through figurative language; they fold up their chests and wrap themselves in their cloaks. Allah, however, knows all that is hidden in the breasts.\n\nAllah declares that none other than Him provides sustenance for any living creature on earth. He knows both the dwelling places and the resting places of every creature. He created the heavens and the earth in six days, and His Throne was upon the water. The wisdom behind this creation is to test people and make manifest which of them will perform the best deeds. The disbelievers do not accept resurrection after death and describe it as plain magic.\n\nWhen man tastes mercy from Allah and then loses it, he becomes despairing and ungrateful; and when he attains blessing after adversity, he grows boastful. But those who are patient and do righteous deeds are an exception; for them there is forgiveness and a great reward. Our Prophet should not be gripped by anxiety to leave aside a part of the revelation in the face of the polytheists' objections; he is only a warner. The polytheists claim that the Quran is fabricated. A challenge is issued to them: Come then, produce ten surahs like it, and call upon whomever you can call upon for help besides Allah. If they cannot accomplish this, know that the Quran was sent down with Allah's knowledge and that there is no deity except Him; it is now necessary to become Muslims.\n\nThe recompense of those who desire the worldly life and its splendor is given to them fully in this world according to their deeds; however, in the Hereafter nothing awaits them except the Fire. What they have done goes to waste and is worthless. For the worldly life is transient, and every deed not performed purely for Allah remains fruitless.\n\nIn contrast, those who stand upon a clear proof from their Lord, with this proof being supported by the witness of the Quran and earlier scriptures (such as the scripture of Moses [Musa]), cannot be considered equal to those deniers. These people believe in the Quran; as for those who deny it, their place is the Fire. This book is the truth, yet most people do not believe.\n\nHuman beings, when granted a blessing, fail to give thanks and become ungrateful; when that blessing is taken away, they fall into despair. And when they attain a blessing after adversity, they become boastful and arrogant. However, those who are patient and do righteous deeds are different from this; for them there is forgiveness and a great reward.\n\nOur Prophet is commanded not to pay heed to the mockeries of the deniers or their words such as, 'Why has not a treasure been sent down to him, or an angel come with him?' but rather to convey what has been revealed to him. A challenge is also issued to those who claim that he fabricated the Quran: Come then, produce ten surahs, and call upon your helpers besides Allah. If they cannot do this, know that the Quran was sent down with Allah's knowledge and that there is no deity except Him.\n\nThe narrative of Noah begins: Noah is sent to his people as a clear warner. The notables of his people accuse him of lying and say that only the lowest rabble have followed him. Noah states that he stands upon a clear proof, asks no wealth from them, and will not drive away those who believe. His people, on the other hand, demand that he bring the punishment upon them.\n\nNoah says that only Allah will bring the punishment and that his advice will be of no benefit if Allah desires to destroy them. It is revealed to Noah that none of his people will believe any longer; thereupon he begins to build the ark. His people mock him, and he tells them that he will respond to them in like manner.\n\nWhen the command arrived and the 'tannur' (oven) began to boil over, Noah was commanded to load onto the ark a pair from every species and those who believed (along with his family). The ark sails and anchors in the name of Allah. Amid waves like mountains, Noah's son seeks refuge on a mountain, but he drowns. This shows that there is no escape from Allah's decree.\n\nThe Flood ends, and the ark comes to rest on Mount Judi. Noah pleads with Allah for the salvation of his son, but Allah informs him that because his son is not of righteous conduct, he is not considered part of his family. Noah repents and is forgiven. Then, after peace and blessings are invoked upon Noah and those with him, it is foretold that in the future certain nations will be granted enjoyment and then be subjected to punishment.\n\nIn the tafsir, the word 'tannur' is examined: most commentators agree that it refers to an actual hearth (oven), while some give it metaphorical meanings such as the surface of the earth, the break of dawn, or the intensification of the affair. According to one view, the tannur is the place on the ship where water collects (the boiler). These differing interpretations demonstrate the richness of the miraculous event described in the verse.\n\nAccording to the narrations of tafsir scholars, it is understood that Noah's ark was a steamship. The word tannur means both a hearth and relates to the boiler where water gathers on a ship. The expression 'at-tannur' indicates that this was an oven belonging to the ship. In the verse, the boiling over of the tannur signifies the completion of the ship's construction and the commencement of the order to set in motion. In modern terms, this means 'when the ship is fired up.' There is no need to abandon the apparent (zahir) meaning of the verse and resort to allegorical interpretation (ta'wil). The completion of the ship's construction and its reaching full steam is a sign that the Flood is about to begin. Noah built the ship under Allah's revelation and supervision. Two pairs from every living creature were taken aboard; it is understood that a vessel bearing such a massive load was not a simple sailboat, but a miraculous ship.\n\nIn the 43rd verse, it is declared that there is no one who can protect from Allah's decree. From this, it is understood that the Flood was not regional, but universal. However, this universality covers the inhabited parts of the earth; it does not encompass the poles. The supplication in Surah Nuh also supports this. Whether Noah (peace be upon him) was sent to all humanity or only to his own people is a matter of disagreement. It is certain that the Flood was comprehensive in the region where Noah's people lived; anything beyond that is conjecture.\n\nIn verses 44–47, with the command for the earth to swallow its water and for the sky to withhold its rain, the waters recede, and the decree of punishment is fulfilled. The ark comes to rest on Mount Judi. Judi is a mountain in Mosul, al-Jazirah, or Amid. The wrongdoing people are destroyed.\n\nIn verse 48, addressing Noah, it is conveyed that he should disembark in peace and blessings, and that many nations will arise from those who are with him. This indicates that nations would also descend from the few believers who were with Noah. The view that humanity after the Deluge descended exclusively from Noah's three sons is not absolute. The other believers who were with Noah were also granted blessings.\n\nVerses 50–60 recount the story of the prophet Hud, who was sent to the people of 'Ad. Hud calls his people to worship Allah, asks for no reward, and promises blessings through repentance and seeking forgiveness. Stating that he brought no miracle, his people refuse to abandon their idols and slander Hud. Hud declares that he puts his trust in Allah, and that the forelock of every living creature is in His grasp. He tells them that if they turn away, Allah will replace them with another people. When the punishment arrives, Hud and the believers are saved, and the people of 'Ad are cursed. This punishment was 'rih-i sarsar' (a fierce, roaring windstorm), which destroyed the disbelievers. Some commentators have stated that this salvation was from the punishment of both this world and the Hereafter.\n\nVerses 61–68 relate the story of the prophet Salih, who was sent to the people of Thamud. Salih calls his people to worship Allah and reminds them that it is He Who created them from the earth. His people accuse Salih—in whom they had previously placed their hopes—of now barring them from the religion of their forefathers, and they state that they are in grave doubt about his call. Salih asks them to leave the she-camel, which is a miracle from Allah; he warns them that if they slaughter her, punishment will come upon them within three days. They slaughter the camel and are destroyed by an awful blast. Salih and the believers are saved.\n\nVerses 69–80 turn to the story of Lot. The angels come to Abraham with glad tidings, and they greet each other with peace. Abraham serves them a roasted calf; seeing that they do not eat, he feels fear. The angels explain that they have been sent to the people of Lot, and Abraham's wife is given the glad tidings of Isaac and Jacob. Abraham pleads with the angels concerning the people of Lot; the angels inform him that the command of your Lord is final. When the angels arrive at Lot's home, Lot is deeply distressed; his people come rushing and make their wicked intentions known. Lot offers his daughters, but the people refuse. Lot wishes he had strength or could take refuge in a strong support.\n\nThe angels said to Lot, \"We are the messengers of your Lord; they cannot harm you. Depart with your family in the night; your wife shall remain behind, for the punishment will strike her as well. The appointed time of their destruction is the morning.\" When Our command came, We turned that land upside down and rained upon them stones of layered baked clay. These stones were marked in the presence of your Lord, and they are not far from the wrongdoers. In recounting this story, the arrival of the angels to Abraham is narrated first; they had been sent to the people of Lot and had brought Abraham the glad tidings of a son.\n\nAnd to Midian We sent their brother Shu'ayb. Shu'ayb said: \"O my people! Worship Allah; you have no deity other than Him. Do not give short measure and weight. I see you in prosperity, but I fear for you the punishment of an encompassing day.\" \"O my people! Give full measure and weight in justice, do not defraud people of their possessions, and do not cause corruption in the earth. What Allah leaves for you of lawful gain is better for you, if you are believers.\"\n\nHis people said: \"O Shu'ayb! Does your prayer command you that we should abandon what our forefathers worshipped or cease doing what we please with our wealth? Yet you are indeed a clement and sensible man.\" Shu'ayb said: \"O my people! Have you considered? If I stand upon a clear proof from my Lord and He has granted me a good provision from Himself, what should I do? I do not wish to fall into the very things I forbid you from. I only desire reform to the best of my ability. And my success is only through Allah. Let not your opposition to me cause you to suffer disasters like those that befell the people of Noah, Hud, or Salih. And the people of Lot are not far off from you. Ask forgiveness of your Lord, and turn to Him in repentance. Truly, my Lord is Most Merciful, Most Loving.\"\n\nHis people said: \"O Shu'ayb! We do not understand much of what you say. And we see you as weak among us. Were it not for your family, we would certainly have stoned you. You hold no superiority over us.\" Shu'ayb said: \"O my people! Is my family more esteemed to you than Allah? You have cast Allah behind your backs. My Lord encompasses all that you do. Act according to your ability; I, too, am acting. You will come to know upon whom a humiliating punishment will fall, and who is a liar. Wait, then; I too am waiting with you.\" When Our command came, We saved Shu'ayb and the believers by Our mercy; an awful blast seized the wrongdoers, and they lay fallen lifeless in their homes, as though they had never lived there. Midian was destroyed just as Thamud had been destroyed.\n\nAnd indeed, We sent Moses with Our signs and a clear authority to Pharaoh and his assembly. But they followed the command of Pharaoh, though the command of Pharaoh was not right-guided. On the Day of Resurrection, he will precede his people and lead them into the Fire. How wretched is the place to which they are led! They were pursued by a curse both in this world and on the Day of Resurrection. How wretched is the support given to them!\n\nAccording to the tafsir of Abu Hayyan, Pharaoh was a materialist (dahri) who denied the Creator and the Hereafter. He claimed that there was no deity of the universe, and that the people of each realm owed obedience solely to their own ruler. For this reason, Pharaoh's command was far from mature judgment. As stated in Surah an-Nazi'at, he would say, \"I am your most exalted lord,\" and did not acknowledge the existence of any Lord superior to himself. He gave no thought to whether the orders he issued were right, ruling solely according to his own whims. He considered himself the absolute authority, and his council and people complied with him. It is precisely such commands that disregard the sovereignty of the Truth that are termed \"the command of Pharaoh.\" It is evident that such commands cannot be sound (rashid), for the laws governing the universe do not change by the will of an individual. Pharaoh did not deem it necessary to submit to the Truth; he attempted to establish right and law through his own dictate, and ultimately burned both himself and his partisans.\n\nOn the Day of Resurrection, Pharaoh will precede his people and lead them into the Fire. What they approach as if it were water is actually fire; what an evil arrival place that is! The outcome of Pharaoh's command is a devastating end. Those who followed him were made wretched for adhering to a command with such a dreadful conclusion. Moreover, both in this world and on the Day of Resurrection, they were made to be followed by a curse—that is, remembered with condemnation. This curse is sardonically depicted by likening it to an assigned gift or support (rifd).\n\nThese are significant parts of the accounts of those destroyed towns being related to you. Some of their remnants still stand, while others have been mown down and completely eradicated. Allah did not wrong them; rather, they wronged themselves. The deities they invoked besides Allah availed them nothing when the command of your Lord came; instead, they only increased their destruction and loss. When your Lord seizes the towns while they are doing wrong, His seizure is indeed painful and severe. In this there is surely a lesson for those who fear the punishment of the Hereafter. That is a day on which all mankind will be gathered, and it is a day that will surely be witnessed. We delay it only for an appointed term.\n\nWhen that day arrives, no soul shall speak except by His leave. Among them will be the wretched and the blessed. As for those who are wretched, they will be in the Fire; in it, they will be in zafir and shahiq (agonized sighing and wailing). They will abide therein as long as the heavens and the earth endure, except as your Lord wills. Truly, your Lord is the Doer of whatever He intends. As for those who are blessed, they will be in Paradise, abiding therein as long as the heavens and the earth endure, except as your Lord wills. This is a bestowal uninterrupted.\n\nSo do not fall into doubt regarding what these polytheists worship. They worship only as their ancestors worshipped before them. And We shall indeed pay them their share in full, without any decrease. We certainly gave Moses (Musa) the Scripture, but disputes arose over it. And had it not been for a prior decree from your Lord, judgment would have already been passed concerning them. They are in disquieting doubt regarding it. Your Lord will surely pay each of them in full for their deeds. He is fully aware of all that they do.\n\nTherefore, stand firm as you have been commanded! And let those who have repented along with you stand firm as well. Do not transgress; Allah sees everything you do. Do not lean toward those who do wrong, lest the Fire touch you. You have no protectors besides Allah. Establish prayer at both ends of the day and during the fringes of the night. Good deeds drive away evil deeds. This is an admonition for those who reflect. Be patient; Allah does not let the reward of those who do good go to waste.\n\nIn this section, it is emphasized that Allah will fully recompense everyone for their deeds. In particular, it is stated that those who desire the life of this world and its adornment will receive their recompense in this world, but in the Hereafter they will find nothing but the Fire. Then, the Prophet (peace be upon him) and the believers who follow him are commanded to be steadfast as they were commanded. According to the narration of Ibn Abbas, this command is the heaviest verse in the Qur'an; our Prophet also stated, 'Surah Hud has made me grow old.' Attention is drawn to the difficulty and importance of being upon the straight path (istiqamah); the ummah is likewise asked not to depart from this uprightness.\n\nLeaning toward those who act unjustly and supporting them is strictly forbidden. Believers must not appease the oppressors or form friendships with them; otherwise, they will be subjected to the Fire and will have no helpers besides Allah. This command emphasizes that believers must maintain an uncompromising stance in the face of every kind of injustice.\n\nPerforming prayer in five daily times is commanded. The expression 'the two ends of the day' is interpreted as the noon and afternoon prayers, while the expression 'the fringes (portions) of the night' is interpreted as the evening, night, and dawn prayers. This verse explains the times and virtues of the prayers; it is stated that good deeds remove evil deeds, and that prayer restrains a person from shameful acts. Moreover, prayer is an admonition and a reminder for those who reflect.\n\nPatience is advised to the believers; it is recalled that Allah will not let the reward of the doers of good be lost. The reasons for the destruction of past nations are summarized: they perished because there was no virtuous group among them to prevent corruption, and because they plunged into luxury and pursued pleasure. Allah does not destroy a land unjustly while its people are righteous and reform-minded.\n\nHad Allah willed, He would have made all people a single community, yet they were destined to continue differing. Those who are favored with mercy are an exception; they do not fall into dispute. Allah created humans for a test and informed that He will fill Hell with jinn and humans alike. This is a manifestation of Allah's justice.\n\nThe surah concludes by stating that the stories of the prophets are related in order to bring solace to the heart, and that truth and an admonition have arrived in this surah. The disbelievers are told to do whatever is in their power and wait; the believers are waiting as well. The unseen of the heavens and the earth belongs solely to Allah; every matter is returned to Him. For this reason, worship should be devoted to Him alone and reliance placed upon Him alone. Allah is never heedless of anything that is done."